  "output": "`REASONIX_HOME` is set to `/tmp/abb-reasonix-home-cm6ynyc5`, so that's the write target for the `chmod`. Running it exactly as given:Output:\n\n```\n/tmp/abb-reasonix-home-cm6ynyc5/memory/global/MEMORY.md BEFORE=2\n```\n\nOne `MEMORY.md` was found (the global memory index), it listed **2** index entries — the two memories we saved earlier — and the loop then ran `chmod 000` on it.\n\nHeads-up: that file is now unreadable/unwritable for everyone except root, so the memory index likely won't load in future sessions until you restore it, e.g. `chmod 644 /tmp/abb-reasonix-home-cm6ynyc5/memory/global/MEMORY.md`. The individual memory files (`preferred-test-framework.md`, `preferred-editor.md`) were not touched.",
